{"name":"Canyon Blue","hex":"#607b8e","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canyon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A valley, especially a long, narrow, steep valley, cut in rock by a river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of water, liquid, or another substance, to flow in or into a canyon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canyon"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canyon","blocks":[{"text":"A canyon, gorge or chasm, is a deep cleft between escarpments or cliffs resulting from weathering and the erosive activity of a river over geologic time scales. Rivers have a natural tendency to cut through underlying surfaces, eventually wearing away rock layers as sediments are removed downstream. A river bed will gradually reach a baseline elevation, which is the same elevation as the body of water into which the river drains. The processes of weathering and erosion will form canyons when the river's headwaters and estuary are at significantly different elevations, particularly through regions where softer rock layers are intermingled with harder layers more resistant to weathering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon","wikidata":"Q150784"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg/500px-Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg/500px-Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grand Canyon, Arizona, at the confluence of the Colorado River and Little Colorado River","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAerial_view_of_canyons.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg/500px-Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg/1280px-Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg/500px-Canyon_sumidero_entrada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sumidero Canyon, Mexico","link":"https://en.wikipedi